Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space and let $2^X$ denote the family of all non-empty closed subsets of $X$. The Wijsman topology $\mathcal{T}_{W(d)}$ on $2^X$ is the coarsest topology that makes each distance functional $d(\cdot, A): 2^X \to \mathbb{R}$ (for $A \in 2^X$) continuous; the pair $(2^X, \mathcal{T}_{W(d)})$ is called the Wijsman hyperspace of $(X,d)$.

Recall the following completeness-type ("Amsterdam") properties of a topological space $Y$:

(i) $Y$ is base-compact with respect to an open base $\mathfrak{B}$ if $Y$ is regular and $\bigcap_{F \in \mathcal{F}} \overline{F} \ne \varnothing$ for every centered family $\mathcal{F} \subseteq \mathfrak{B}$ (a family is centered if every finite subfamily has non-empty intersection);

(ii) $Y$ is subcompact with respect to an open base $\mathfrak{B}$ if $Y$ is regular and $\bigcap_{F \in \mathcal{F}} \overline{F} \ne \varnothing$ for every regular filterbase $\mathcal{F} \subseteq \mathfrak{B}$ (a family $\mathcal{F}$ of non-empty open sets is a regular filterbase if for any $U,V \in \mathcal{F}$ there exists $W \in \mathcal{F}$ with $\overline{W} \subseteq U \cap V$);

(iii) $Y$ is pseudocomplete if $Y$ is quasi-regular and possesses a sequence $\{\mathfrak{P}_n : n \in \mathbb{N}\}$ of open $\pi$-bases such that $\bigcap_{n\in\mathbb{N}} V_n \ne \varnothing$ whenever $V_n \in \mathfrak{P}_n$ and $\overline{V_{n+1}} \subseteq V_n$ for each $n$.

Question: If $(X,d)$ is pseudocomplete (respectively, subcompact, base-compact), must its Wijsman hyperspace $(2^X, \mathcal{T}_{W(d)})$ have the corresponding property?

